        <div id="wattage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Many Watts Your Laptop Actually Needs
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start With the Wattage on the Brick</h3>

          <p>
            The single most important number when buying a laptop portable charger is your laptop's charger wattage, and it is printed on the brick. If the bank's USB-C port can deliver at least that much, your laptop charges at full speed. If it delivers less, the laptop charges slowly, holds steady, or still drains while you work. Battery capacity decides how long the bank lasts; port wattage decides whether it keeps up at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wattage Tiers by Laptop Type</h3>

          <p>
            Most laptops fall into clear tiers. A MacBook Air, a Chromebook or a small Windows ultrabook ships with a 30 to 70W charger. Mainstream 14-inch laptops like the 14-inch MacBook Pro, Dell XPS 14 and ThinkPad X1 use 65 to 100W. Larger performance machines such as the 16-inch MacBook Pro or Dell XPS 16 draw 130 to 140W. Gaming laptops often use 180 to 330W barrel chargers.
          </p>

