          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Going Below Your Charger's Rating</h3>

          <p>
            You can go below your charger's rating and still get some use. A 65W bank on a 96W laptop will charge it when the lid is closed or under light work, and slow the drain during heavier work. What you cannot do is expect a 30W phone bank to keep up with any laptop under load. The practical floor for laptop charging is about 45W, and 65W is a better minimum for a 14-inch machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity Is the Second Number</h3>

          <p>
            Capacity is the second number. Laptop batteries typically hold 50 to 100Wh, and a power bank delivers roughly 70 to 85 percent of its rated Wh to the laptop after conversion losses. So a 90Wh bank gives about one full charge of a 70Wh laptop, or close to two charges of a MacBook Air. Under the airline limit, that is about the most anyone can carry, which is why every bank on our list sits between 74 and 99.75Wh except the AC-outlet station.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pd-31" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. USB PD 3.1 and 140W Explained
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">PD 3.0 vs PD 3.1</h3>

          <p>
            USB Power Delivery is the protocol a laptop and charger use to negotiate voltage and current over USB-C. The older PD 3.0 standard tops out at 100W, using 20 volts at 5 amps. PD 3.1 added Extended Power Range, or EPR, which raises voltage to 28V for 140W and higher steps up to 240W. That is the change that made 140W laptop power banks possible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three Things Must Line Up for 140W</h3>

          <p>
            To get 140W, three things must line up: the bank must support PD 3.1 at 140W, the laptop must request it, and the cable must be rated for 240W. A standard 100W USB-C cable will cap charging at 100W even if the bank and laptop both support more. The cable is the most common reason a 140W bank appears to underperform, and 240W cables are inexpensive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not all laptops use PD 3.1. A 16-inch MacBook Pro accepts 140W over USB-C via its MagSafe 3 cable or USB-C, but many Windows laptops that ship with 130 to 140W chargers still cap USB-C input at 100W. Check your laptop's specs for its maximum USB-C charging input before paying for a 140W bank. If it says 100W, a 100W bank charges it just as fast.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Total output vs per-port output</h3>

          <p>
            Read the spec sheet for both numbers. A bank advertised as 145W may deliver 140W from one port but only 145W across all ports combined, so plugging in a phone alongside the laptop steals power from the laptop. Banks with 210W or 300W total output, like the CUKTECH 20 and Anker Prime, can feed a 140W laptop and still charge other devices at speed. If you regularly charge more than one laptop, total output matters more than capacity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="macbook-vs-gaming" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. MacBook vs Windows vs Gaming Laptops
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">MacBooks</h3>

          <p>
            MacBooks are the easiest laptops to power from a bank. Apple Silicon machines are efficient, they charge over standard USB-C PD, and their real-world draw is often well below the charger rating. A MacBook Air drawing 10 to 20W during light work can run for many hours on a 90Wh bank on top of its own battery. Even a 16-inch MacBook Pro only pulls its full 140W during heavy rendering or when charging from low.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Windows Ultrabooks</h3>

          <p>
            Windows ultrabooks are similar, with one catch: check whether the laptop actually charges over USB-C. Most modern models from Dell, Lenovo, HP, Asus and Microsoft do, but some business laptops and older machines only charge through a proprietary barrel plug. Look for a small battery or lightning icon next to the USB-C port, or check the manual. If your laptop does not support USB-C charging, a USB-C bank will not charge it at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gaming Laptops</h3>

          <p>
            Gaming laptops are where power banks fall short. A laptop with a high-end discrete GPU can draw 150 to 250W or more while gaming, which is more than any USB-C bank can supply. Some gaming laptops accept 100 or 140W over USB-C, but only to charge while idle or for light tasks; under a gaming load they still drain the battery. Be realistic: a power bank extends a gaming laptop's battery for browsing and work, not for gaming.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need to game away from an outlet, the honest answer is an AC-outlet power station running the laptop's normal charger. Even then, a 245Wh unit gives only about an hour and a half of heavy gaming. For longer sessions, see our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a>, where larger units provide several times the runtime but weigh 20 pounds or more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ac-outlet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. AC-Outlet Laptop Banks: When You Need One
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What an AC Outlet Adds</h3>

          <p>
            A power bank with an AC outlet contains a small inverter that converts battery power to household-style AC, so you can plug in any laptop charger. That is the only way to run a barrel-plug laptop or a gaming laptop's full charger away from the wall. It is also useful for camera battery chargers, drone chargers and other devices that come with a wall plug.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Cost: Efficiency and Weight</h3>

          <p>
            The cost is efficiency and weight. Converting DC to AC, then back to DC inside your laptop charger, wastes roughly 10 to 15 percent of the energy compared with direct USB-C. Small pocket banks with AC outlets used to exist, but most have been discontinued or offer only 60 to 100W, which barely covers a mainstream laptop. In 2026, the practical AC options are small power stations like the EcoFlow River 3.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Laptop Power at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">100W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">USB PD 3.0 maximum</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">140W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">PD 3.1 bank maximum today</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">99.75Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Largest carry-on bank on our list</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">160Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Absolute airline maximum</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">AC Stations Cannot Fly</h3>

          <p>
            Those stations cannot fly. Any unit over 160Wh is banned from passenger aircraft entirely, so an AC-outlet station is for cars, job sites, campsites and desks, not airports. For more on picking a small station by brand, our <a href="/best-ecoflow-power-station/">EcoFlow</a> and <a href="/best-anker-power-station/">Anker power station</a> guides compare the current lineups.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Confirm You Need AC First</h3>

          <p>
            Before buying an AC unit, confirm that your laptop truly needs it. If it charges over USB-C, a USB-C bank is lighter, cheaper, more efficient and flyable. Reserve AC stations for barrel-plug machines, gaming sessions, and setups where you also need to run non-USB devices like a monitor or a printer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="airline-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Airline Limits for Laptop Power Banks
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 100Wh and 160Wh Limits</h3>

          <p>
            Laptop banks are where airline rules start to bite. Under FAA and ICAO rules, power banks up to 100Wh may go in carry-on bags without approval. Banks from 101 to 160Wh require airline approval, and you can carry at most two. Anything over 160Wh is forbidden on passenger planes. No power bank may go in checked luggage, ever.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Laptop Banks Stop at 100Wh</h3>

          <p>
            That 100Wh ceiling is why laptop banks top out around 25,000 to 26,800mAh. The Anker Prime at 99.75Wh is effectively the largest bank you can bring on a typical flight without asking. Larger banks in the 101 to 160Wh range exist, but approval at check-in is inconsistent and some airlines simply refuse. For anyone who flies regularly, a sub-100Wh bank is the only sensible choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">In-Flight Use Rules</h3>

          <p>
            In-flight use has become stricter since 2025. Southwest requires banks to stay visible while in use, and several international carriers, including Emirates and a number of Asian airlines, ban using or charging power banks onboard entirely. Mainland China also requires a 3C certification mark on power banks for domestic flights. If you plan to work through a long-haul flight on bank power, check your airline's current policy first, because on some carriers you will have to rely on the seat outlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Your Laptop's Own Battery</h3>

          <p>
            Your laptop's own battery is separate. Laptops with installed batteries, which are almost always under 100Wh, can go in carry-on bags under the device rules, and power banks are counted as spare batteries. Keep both in the cabin and keep the bank where you can reach it. For more on choosing a bank for flying with a phone, see our <a href="/best-power-bank-for-travel/">best power banks for travel</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity-runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Capacity and Runtime Math
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Basic Formula</h3>

          <p>
            To estimate how much extra time a bank gives you, take its rated watt-hours, multiply by about 0.8 for conversion losses, and divide by your laptop's average draw while working. A 90Wh bank gives about 72Wh usable. A MacBook Air averaging 12W during web and document work gets roughly six extra hours. A Windows ultrabook averaging 20W gets about three and a half hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heavier Workloads</h3>

          <p>
            Heavier work changes the picture fast. Video editing or compiling code on a 16-inch MacBook Pro can average 40 to 60W, so the same 72Wh adds about an hour and a half. A gaming laptop drawing 150W through an AC station would drain even a 245Wh unit in roughly an hour and a quarter. The table in the first section of this guide matches laptops to banks by peak need; this math tells you how long they will last.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Charge Then Unplug, or Run From the Bank</h3>

          <p>
            Charging the laptop's battery versus running it directly makes little difference in total energy, but it does change behavior. If you charge a laptop to full from the bank and then unplug, you avoid the bank heating up in your bag. If you leave it connected while working, the laptop draws from the bank first and preserves its own battery cycles. Both approaches work; choose whichever fits your setup.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two Banks Beat One Oversize Bank</h3>

          <p>
            If you need more than one laptop charge per day away from outlets, two banks beat one oversize bank for flying. Two 90Wh banks fly without approval, while a single 150Wh bank needs airline sign-off. For desk-style use or car trips, a larger power station is more cost-effective per watt-hour; our guide on <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">what size power station you need</a> walks through that sizing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cables-chargers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cables, Wall Chargers and Recharge Speed
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Right Cable</h3>

          <p>
            The cable is part of the system. For 60W charging, nearly any USB-C cable works. For 100W, you need a cable rated for 5 amps, usually marked 100W. For 140W, you need a 240W-rated cable that supports PD 3.1 EPR. Buy a short, quality cable from the bank's maker or another reputable brand, and keep it with the bank so you never borrow a weaker one by accident.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recharge Speed and Wall Chargers</h3>

          <p>
            Recharge speed separates flagship laptop banks from budget ones. A 90Wh bank that accepts 65W input refills in about two hours, while one that accepts 100W or more can refill in about an hour. That matters if you get a short window at an outlet between meetings or flights. To take advantage, you need a wall charger at least as powerful as the bank's input rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            A compact GaN wall charger with 100W or more and two or three USB-C ports is the natural companion to a laptop bank. It recharges the laptop and the bank at the same time from one outlet, and most are rated 100 to 240V for international use with only a plug adapter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pass-Through Charging</h3>

          <p>
            Pass-through charging, where the bank charges devices while plugged into the wall, is supported on most current laptop banks. It is useful in a hotel or office with limited outlets, though it can make the bank run warm. Do not leave a bank on pass-through permanently as a desk UPS; constant heat and full charge shorten its life. For true desk backup, a small LiFePO4 station is the better tool, as covered in our <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-life" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Safety, Heat and Battery Life
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat and Warning Signs</h3>

          <p>
            Laptop banks push more current than phone banks, so they run warmer under load, especially at 100W and above. A warm bank is normal; a bank that becomes too hot to hold comfortably is not. Charge it on a hard surface, not on a bed or inside a closed bag, and stop using any bank that swells, smells of solvent or shows a cracked case.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Avoid No-Name High-Wattage Banks</h3>

          <p>
            This category is full of cheap high-wattage banks with inflated capacity claims and unclear certifications. A bank pushing 140W through poorly made cells or weak protection circuits is a real fire risk, which is why we stick to established brands like Anker, UGREEN, CUKTECH, Baseus and EcoFlow. Treat any no-name listing claiming 40,000mAh and 200W at a bargain price with suspicion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stretching Battery Life</h3>

          <p>
            Most lithium-ion power banks are rated for a few hundred to about 1,000 cycles before capacity drops noticeably. To stretch that, avoid storing the bank fully charged or fully drained for long periods, keep it out of hot cars, and top it up every few months if it sits unused. LiFePO4 units like the River 3 last several times longer but weigh much more per watt-hour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Double Duty for Outages</h3>

          <p>
            If you also want a bank for home emergencies, a laptop bank does double duty well: it can keep phones, a router and a laptop going for a day or two. Our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-charger-for-power-outages/">best portable chargers for power outages</a> covers outage-specific picks and how to keep a bank ready for when the grid goes down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size power bank do I need for a laptop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the bank's USB-C output to your laptop's charger wattage first, then pick capacity. Most people should buy a 20,000 to 26,000mAh bank, about 74 to 99Wh, which gives roughly one full laptop charge and stays under airline limits. A MacBook Air gets close to two charges from a 90Wh bank; a 16-inch MacBook Pro gets about one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a power bank charge a MacBook Pro 16?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, at full speed if the bank supports 140W over USB PD 3.1 and you use a 240W-rated cable. The Anker Prime, CUKTECH 20, UGREEN Nexode and Anker 737 all do. A 100W bank also charges it, just more slowly under heavy load. Expect about one full charge from a 90 to 99Wh bank after conversion losses.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I charge a gaming laptop with a power bank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only partly. Gaming laptops can draw 150 to 250W or more under load, which exceeds any USB-C bank. Models that accept USB-C charging will charge at idle or during light work from a 100 to 140W bank. For gaming away from an outlet, use an AC-outlet power station that runs the laptop's normal charger.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I take a laptop power bank on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in carry-on only, if it is 100Wh or less, which covers banks up to about 27,000mAh. Banks from 101 to 160Wh need airline approval, with a limit of two. Over 160Wh is banned. Some airlines also restrict using power banks during flight, so check your carrier's current policy before relying on one to work onboard.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 65W power bank enough for a laptop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most ultrabooks, MacBook Airs and Chromebooks, yes. A 65W bank charges them at full or near-full speed. For a 14-inch MacBook Pro or similar 90 to 100W laptop, 65W will charge it during light work but may not keep up under heavy load. For 16-inch performance laptops, step up to a 140W bank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a power bank with an AC outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if your laptop charges through a barrel plug instead of USB-C, or you need to run a gaming laptop's full charger. AC outlets waste 10 to 15 percent of energy in conversion and add weight, and the units that offer real AC power are generally too large to fly. For USB-C laptops, a USB-C bank is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my laptop charging slowly from a power bank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually one of three reasons: the bank's port delivers less wattage than the laptop wants, the cable is rated for only 60 or 100W, or other devices connected to the bank are sharing its total output. Check the bank's display for live wattage, use a 240W-rated cable, and unplug other devices to see if speed improves.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a phone bank for a laptop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 20W or 30W bank cannot keep a laptop charged under load; at best it slows the drain. Check the wattage on your laptop's charger and buy a bank whose USB-C port matches it, or at least reaches 65W for a 14-inch machine. Capacity alone does not make a bank laptop-capable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a 100W cable with a 140W bank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 140W PD 3.1 bank will only deliver 100W through a standard cable. If your 16-inch MacBook Pro charges slower than expected, the cable is the likeliest cause. Buy a 240W-rated USB-C cable, keep it with the bank, and confirm the wattage on the bank's display.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a gaming laptop will charge while gaming</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most gaming laptops draw 150 to 250W or more under load, beyond what any USB-C bank can supply. Even models that accept USB-C charging still drain the battery while gaming. Use a USB-C bank for work and browsing, and an AC-outlet station for actual gaming sessions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a bank over 100Wh to fly with</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Banks between 101 and 160Wh need airline approval that is often refused at check-in, and anything over 160Wh cannot fly at all. Every bank on our pocketable list sits under 100Wh for that reason. If you need more energy on a trip, carry two sub-100Wh banks instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring whether the laptop charges over USB-C</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some business, workstation and older laptops only charge through a barrel plug and will not accept power from a USB-C bank at all. Check for a charging icon next to the USB-C port or read the manual before buying. If it is barrel-only, you need an AC-outlet unit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

